// PINS A KNOWN BUG (issue #781). THIS TEST IS EXPECTED TO GO RED, and
// when it does the fix is to rewrite it, not to re-record it.
//
// `Threads.sleep` is declared in `std/env/env.zen` and lowered NOWHERE:
// `sleep` appears exactly once in the whole of `src/`, on line 153 of
// that file, and it is the declaration. Unlike `env.args<T>()` -- the
// other bodiless capability in the same struct, refused at the call by
// `must-fail/codegen/env_args_is_not_lowered_yet` -- this one COMPILES
// CLEAN. `zen build` exits 0, `cc` is happy, and the program dies with
// SIGSEGV the moment it reaches the call.
//
// SO THE EXPECTATION IS THE CRASH, written down. A capability that
// compiles and then faults is the worst of the three outcomes and the
// only one no gate in this tree was watching: `make build` sees exit 0,
// the corpus never called it, and nothing was red. Recording it here
// makes the silence audible -- and makes the day somebody gives `sleep`
// a floor a RED RUN rather than a thing nobody notices.
//
// WHAT THE TWO ASSERTED FACTS ARE:
//
//   exit 139 -- 128 + SIGSEGV(11), stable across repeated runs.
//   stdout EMPTY -- and that is the second half of the harm. "before"
//   is printed BEFORE the call; the crash takes the process down with
//   stdout still buffered, so a program that had already answered
//   loses its answer. A caller that saw partial output would at least
//   know where it stopped.
//
// THE FIX MAKES THIS FILE RED TWO WAYS AT ONCE: `before` and `after`
// both arrive on stdout and the exit code becomes 0. Replace this file
// with the ordinary test then -- sleep(0) returns, sleep(n) does not
// come back before n, and `env.clock.since_start` is the witness.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("before");
    env.threads.sleep(1);
    println("after");
    Ok(0);
}
